# Tools and Toolsets

This tutorial gives an AI Agent tools in the three ways Jido supports: Actions, Flows, and trusted runtime sources. It then narrows them for one request and loads a `SKILL.md` file as a tool. A local client shows which tools the model receives.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "mikehostetler/new_jido", branch: "main"}
])
```

## Declare Action and Flow tools

An Action or Flow becomes a tool when the Agent's `tools` block names it. The tool name is its `name`, its description goes to the model, and its input schema becomes the tool's parameters. Give every tool a clear description.

```elixir
defmodule ToolsGuide.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up one order by ID",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{order_id: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context), do: {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, total_cents: 4_200}}
end

defmodule ToolsGuide.ApplyDiscount do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "apply_discount",
    description: "Compute a discounted total",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer(), percent: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{total_cents: total, percent: percent}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total_cents: div(total * (100 - percent), 100)}}
  end
end

defmodule ToolsGuide.DiscountFlow do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "discount_order",
    description: "Look up an order and compute its 10 percent discount",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{order_id: Jido.Joi.string()})

  flow do
    step "order", action: ToolsGuide.LookupOrder, params: %{order_id: input(:order_id)}

    step "discount",
      action: ToolsGuide.ApplyDiscount,
      params: %{total_cents: result("order", :total_cents), percent: value(10)}

    output result("discount")
  end
end

defmodule ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "orders_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct

    tools do
      action ToolsGuide.LookupOrder
      flow ToolsGuide.DiscountFlow
    end
  end
end

ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent.explain().tools
```

The cell returns the two modules in declaration order. A tools list must not repeat a module or a name. Compilation fails if it does. Use `action` for an Action and `flow` for a Flow. Jido finds the real kind from the module.

## Inspect the Toolset

`Jido.AI.Toolset` works on the list directly. `Jido.AI.Toolset.definitions/1` builds what the model receives.

```elixir
tools = ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent.explain().tools

:ok = Jido.AI.Toolset.validate(tools)
{:error, :duplicate_tools} = Jido.AI.Toolset.validate(tools ++ tools)
{:ok, definitions} = Jido.AI.Toolset.definitions(tools)

Enum.map(definitions, &{&1.name, &1.description})
```

The cell returns `[{"lookup_order", "Look up one order by ID"}, {"discount_order", "Look up an order and compute its 10 percent discount"}]`.

The definitions have no working callback. Tools run as graph nodes in the Flow, never from inside ReqLLM. Definitions are strict, and their JSON Schema lists a field with a default as required, so the model must send it.

## Record the tools each request sends

This client calls the first tool it is offered, once, and stores the tool names and tool choice.

```elixir
defmodule ToolsGuide.LocalModel do
  @behaviour Jido.AI.InferenceClient

  def start, do: Agent.start_link(fn -> [] end, name: __MODULE__)
  def offered, do: Agent.get(__MODULE__, &List.first/1)

  @impl true
  def generate_text(model, context, options) do
    tools = options[:tools] || []
    offer = %{tools: Enum.map(tools, & &1.name), tool_choice: options[:tool_choice]}
    Agent.update(__MODULE__, &[offer | &1])

    message =
      case {context |> ReqLLM.Context.to_list() |> List.last(), tools} do
        {%{role: :user}, [tool | _others]} ->
          call = ReqLLM.ToolCall.new("call-1", tool.name, arguments_for(tool.name))
          ReqLLM.Context.assistant("", tool_calls: [call])

        {%{role: :tool, metadata: %{tool_output: output}}, _tools} ->
          ReqLLM.Context.assistant("Tool result: #{JSON.encode!(output)}")

        _other ->
          ReqLLM.Context.assistant("No tool was needed.")
      end

    {:ok,
     %ReqLLM.Response{
       id: "local",
       model: model,
       context: context,
       message: message,
       finish_reason: if(message.tool_calls in [nil, []], do: :stop, else: :tool_calls),
       usage: %{input_tokens: 4, output_tokens: 3}
     }}
  end

  defp arguments_for("load_skill"), do: ~s({"name":"refund-policy"})
  defp arguments_for("exchange_rate"), do: ~s({"currency":"EUR"})
  defp arguments_for(_order_tool), do: ~s({"order_id":"ORD-5"})

  @impl true
  def evaluation_models, do: []

  for {name, arity} <- Jido.AI.InferenceClient.behaviour_info(:callbacks),
      {name, arity} not in [generate_text: 3, evaluation_models: 0] do
    @impl true
    def unquote(name)(unquote_splicing(Macro.generate_arguments(arity, __MODULE__))),
      do: {:error, :not_supported}
  end
end

{:ok, _local_model} = ToolsGuide.LocalModel.start()

run_orders = fn params, extra_context ->
  agent = ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent.instantiate!(id: "orders-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")

  result =
    ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent.cmd(
      agent,
      ToolsGuide.OrdersAgent.request_signal!(params),
      context: Map.merge(%{jido_ai_inference_client: ToolsGuide.LocalModel}, extra_context)
    )

  case result do
    {:ok, _agent, [%{signal: reply}]} -> reply.data
    {:error, error} -> {:error, error}
  end
end

reply = run_orders.(%{input: "Show order ORD-5."}, %{})

{reply.output, hd(reply.tool_attempts).tool.kind}
```

The model called `lookup_order`, the first tool. The cell returns `{~s(Tool result: {"order_id":"ORD-5","total_cents":4200}), :action}`.

## Narrow tools for one request

`tool_policy` can only remove tools from the Agent's list. It never adds one. `visible` selects tools, and `hidden` removes them.

```elixir
visible = run_orders.(%{input: "Discount it.", tool_policy: %{visible: ["discount_order"]}}, %{})
flow_attempt = hd(visible.tool_attempts)

disabled = run_orders.(%{input: "Answer directly.", tool_policy: %{requirement: :disabled}}, %{})
disabled_offer = ToolsGuide.LocalModel.offered()

unknown = run_orders.(%{input: "Delete it.", tool_policy: %{visible: ["delete_order"]}}, %{})

{
  visible.output,
  flow_attempt.tool.kind,
  disabled.output,
  disabled_offer,
  elem(unknown, 0)
}
```

| Result | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Flow tool output | `~s(Tool result: {"total_cents":3780})` |
| Tool kind | `:flow` |
| Disabled output | `"No tool was needed."` |
| Disabled offer | `%{tools: [], tool_choice: :none}` |
| Unknown name | `:error`, before any model call |

## Require tool use

`tool_policy.requirement` lowers to a provider tool choice and an output check.

| Requirement | Tool choice sent | Final answer accepted |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:optional` | not set | Always |
| `:required` | `:required` before the first tool round, then `:auto` | After at least one tool round |
| `:per_turn` | `:required` on every call | Never, while any tool is selected |
| `:disabled` | `:none` | Always, and no tools are offered |

With `:per_turn`, the output guard rejects every final answer while a tool is selected. The request can only end as `:blocked`, either with reason code `:output` after a final answer or `:limits` when tool calls run out. Use `:required` when you want one tool round and then an answer. A `:required` or `:per_turn` requirement with no selected tool returns an error before any model call.

```elixir
required = run_orders.(%{input: "Show order ORD-5.", tool_policy: %{requirement: :required}}, %{})

{required.status, required.tool_rounds, ToolsGuide.LocalModel.offered().tool_choice}
```

The cell returns `{:completed, 1, :auto}`. The last value is the tool choice on the second call.

## Add tools at run time

Implement `Jido.AI.ToolSource` to load tools from something the Agent definition cannot name, such as a tenant's connector. A trusted host adds the source to the Action context under `:jido_ai_tool_sources`. Request data cannot add a source. Source tools come after the Agent's own tools, and `tool_policy` can narrow them.

```elixir
defmodule ToolsGuide.RatesSource do
  @behaviour Jido.AI.ToolSource

  @impl true
  def list_tools(_config, _context) do
    {:ok,
     [
       %{
         name: "exchange_rate",
         description: "Return one exchange rate",
         parameter_schema: %{
           "type" => "object",
           "properties" => %{"currency" => %{"type" => "string"}},
           "required" => ["currency"],
           "additionalProperties" => false
         }
       }
     ]}
  end

  @impl true
  def run_tool(%{name: "exchange_rate", arguments: arguments}, %{base: base}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{base: base, currency: arguments["currency"], rate: 0.92}}
  end
end

source_reply =
  run_orders.(
    %{input: "What is the rate?", tool_policy: %{visible: ["exchange_rate"]}},
    %{jido_ai_tool_sources: [{ToolsGuide.RatesSource, %{base: "USD"}}]}
  )

{source_reply.output, hd(source_reply.tool_attempts).tool}
```

The output holds the rate, and the attempt records `%{kind: :source, name: "exchange_rate", source: "Elixir.ToolsGuide.RatesSource"}`.

The source specification and each definition must be portable data, because a durable Turn can store them. A source with a PID or function in its config fails to load, and the request returns an error.

## Load skills

`Jido.AI.Skill` is a tool source that serves `SKILL.md` files. A root holds one directory per skill, and each file needs `name` and `description` frontmatter. The name must match its directory, use lowercase words joined by hyphens, and be at most 64 bytes. The description has a 1,024-byte limit.

```elixir
root = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "tools-guide-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
File.mkdir_p!(Path.join(root, "refund-policy"))

File.write!(Path.join([root, "refund-policy", "SKILL.md"]), """
---
name: refund-policy
description: Apply the refund policy to an order.
---
Refund within 30 days of delivery.
""")

{:ok, [%{name: "refund-policy", description: "Apply the refund policy to an order."}]} =
  Jido.AI.Skill.discover([root])

skill_reply =
  run_orders.(
    %{input: "Can I refund it?", tool_policy: %{visible: ["load_skill"]}},
    %{jido_ai_tool_sources: [Jido.AI.Skill.source([root])]}
  )

File.rm_rf!(root)

hd(skill_reply.tool_attempts).output["instructions"]
```

The cell returns `"Refund within 30 days of delivery.\n"`. The source adds one `load_skill` tool. Its description lists the available skills. A call returns the Markdown body as a tool result, and the model reads it in the next round. Jido does not run scripts or read other files in a skill directory.

